{
 "id": "1903-angier-most",
 "name": "Most pleasing division of a line (Angier 1903 unequal division)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Part I of Roswell Parker Angier's experiment \"The aesthetics of unequal division\" (Harvard Psychological Studies 1, 1903, pp. 541–561). On a dark screen a lit horizontal line (160 mm in the original) is shown with a small divider. The participant first moves the divider over the whole line, then stops it at the place that divides the line unequally in the most pleasing way, once in the left half and once in the right half; a second round starts from the other end. Each answer is the divider position in millimetres of the 160 mm line (from the left end), so results compare directly with Angier's Table I (overall averages 60 and 59 mm from the ends, golden section 61.1 mm) and with his point that individuals keep to their own zones of preference. Differences from the original study: (1) the line is drawn on screen (its pixel length is stored with each answer) instead of a 160 x 1.5 mm slit lit from behind in a darkened room, viewed from about four feet; (2) four judgments per person instead of 72 over many sessions; (3) the wider and shorter lines and later parts of the paper are left out; gender, age and art training are asked at the end. Public domain source.",
